  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Designing the Implementation**\n\nI\'m now diving into the implementation phase. I\'ve decided to carefully design the `/server.ts` file, approaching it modularly. My focus is on ensuring each endpoint is perfectly crafted. Initially, I\'m considering the structural endpoints required by the frontend, particularly the authentication routes. I\'m starting with `/api/auth/login` to begin with.\n\n\n**Defining Authentication & Roles**\n\nI\'ve finalized the authentication routes. Login will issue a session user with a pseudo-token, containing email and role (OWNER/EDITOR). I\'ve built the register route for internal employees, adding users to `database.json`. `/api/auth/me` will decode the token, returning the user\'s role. I\'m now moving on to the client and device routes, considering the structure of the data and required access controls.\n\n\n**Planning API Routes**\n\nI\'ve scoped out the core routes for work orders (`/api/ordens-servico`), including GET, POST, PUT (for updates and status changes), and DELETE (with role-based access). I\'m considering the sequence generation for new OS entries. I\'ve also outlined routes for parts and inventory management, including deduction (`/api/parts/deduct`). I\'m now pausing on the Bling API integration, awaiting developments there.\n\n\n**Implementing Bling Sync**\n\nI\'m now detailing Bling integration, focusing on a robust real and simulated flow. The real flow will use the API key to send data to Bling, with Axios, at the right endpoint. The simulation will provide an interactive debugging experience. I\'m building in timeout handling, exponential backoff, and a circuit breaker for resilience, with clear state tracing for the user. I\'m also planning for SEFAZ rejections, providing simulation toggle conditions.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 48
      nanos: 247583751
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
